          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on what you have described.** The red-flag table did not match your description. That is not a clearance. Breathlessness on exertion can have heart, lung, or blood causes, and only a clinician can sort them out. Arrange a routine appointment soon, and seek urgent care sooner if any of the signs below appear.\n\n**Seek emergency care now if you have:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly\n- Chest pain or pressure together with breathlessness\n- Being unable to speak in full sentences\n- Blue or gray lips, tongue, face, or fingertips\n- Fainting, or confusion or unusual drowsiness\n\n*(Source: red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty)*\n\n**Seek prompt medical assessment if you notice:**\n- Breathlessness that wakes you at night or does not settle when you sit up. This is a heart-failure warning sign.\n- Chest discomfort during exertion. Breathlessness on effort can be the only symptom of heart disease, especially in women, older adults, and people with diabetes. *(Source: coronary-artery-disease)*\n- Rapid weight gain over a few days, or new swelling. *(Source: heart-failure)*\n- A very fast or irregular pulse, especially at rest. *(Source: heart-failure, iron-deficiency-anemia)*\n- Coughing up blood, or pink frothy sputum. *(Sources: copd, heart-failure)*\n- Black tarry stools or vomiting blood. *(Source: iron-deficiency-anemia)*\n\n**Main possibilities to raise with a clinician, grouped by body system:**\n- **Heart:** coronary artery disease and heart failure can both cause breathlessness on exertion.\n- **Lungs:** chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a possibility, particularly with a smoking history or a persistent cough.\n- **Blood:** iron-deficiency anemia can reduce the blood's capacity to carry oxygen, which makes effort feel harder.\n\nThe analysis tool did not group your symptoms into a body system and did not rank these, so this list is a set of questions to ask, not a diagnosis. Your clinician can check your heart rhythm, lungs, and blood count.\n\n**Limits of this answer:** My search for more detail on exertional breathlessness failed, so I could not retrieve further guidance. Stress and anxiety can also raise breathing and heart rate during exertion, but that is not a reason to dismiss new symptoms. Please have a clinician evaluate them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
